$$List vs Map in Java
>“List stores elements in a collection and allows duplicate values, while Map stores data in key-value pairs where each key must be unique.”

List vs Map in Java

List                                                              Map
Stores elements/values                                            Stores key-value pairs
Allows duplicate elements                                       	Keys must be unique
Maintains order depending on implementation	                      Order depends on implementation
Uses index to access elements                                   	Uses key to access values
list.get(0)	                                                      map.get(key)
Examples: ArrayList, LinkedList	                                  Examples: HashMap, TreeMap

List:
List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();

list.add("Amit");
list.add("Rahul");
list.add("Amit");


Map:
Map<Integer, String> map = new HashMap<>();

map.put(1, "Amit");
map.put(2, "Rahul");
///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

ArrayList vs HashSet in Java

>“ArrayList allows duplicate elements, maintains insertion order, and provides index-based access, whereas HashSet stores only unique elements and does not guarantee insertion order.”
ArrayList:
ArrayList<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();

list.add(10);
list.add(20);
list.add(10);

System.out.println(list);


HashSet:
HashSet<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();

set.add(10);
set.add(20);
set.add(10);

System.out.println(set);



List vs Map in Java
List                                                   	Map
Stores elements/values	                                Stores key-value pairs
Allows duplicate elements                              	Keys must be unique
Maintains order depending on implementation            	Order depends on implementation
Uses index to access elements                         	Uses key to access values
list.get(0)                                           	map.get(key)
Examples: ArrayList, LinkedList                       	Examples: HashMap, TreeMap

